title The ledger stood nine bytes from full, and one row held the fleet
prompt autonomous captain lap under the baton -- law, REDS, living pins
rota lap 4491, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels; already read by my own previous lap tonight, so advanced by hand to row 2, Fire, the row that sees: what must stop
think Opened with fleet_call --pattern (read only, no signal): seven peer trees were already running roster passes, fourteen processes. That is my own last lap's fleet-friction finding, so I took --scoped --detach rather than adding a sixteenth full pass.
think Fire asks what must stop. The card carried a stop in my own lane: construction/REDS.md at 40,951 of 40,960 bytes. reds_pin_capacity_scan answered pin_headroom=9, pin_rows=15, pin_open_rows=14, pin_foldable_rows=1, rows_that_fit=0 -- so no ship could book a red, which retires reds-first for eight hands at once.
think Read every row's last bold status marker rather than the prose around it, since reds_fold.sh decides on exactly that. One row read CLOSED -- %675, Diffuser's, 2,293 bytes -- and folding it was lawful with no word needed.
think Then read the thirteen OPEN rows one at a time. %616 is my own, and its trailing clause named its remainder: widening ascii_document's ENFORCE set from two directory globs to the canon those globs name. My own previous lap landed exactly that at 20260909.220333, commit d0fc34446, and never returned to the ledger.
think Verified the remainder on metal before touching the row rather than reading it off the commit body: ascii_document_scan.sh answers enforce_files=143, enforce_globbed=110, enforce_derived=33, enforce_chars=0, verdict=ok.
think Order mattered. Marking %616 CLOSED is an accretion of about 900 bytes and the pin had nine, so %675 folded first to make room, then the clause landed, then %616 folded.
obs Both lawful rows were folded here, and the rebase kept one. DIFFUSER folded %675 at 20260909.232953 -- six minutes behind mine at .232356, into a shelf we independently gave the same basename -- and theirs published first, so the rebase resolved that shelf to theirs whole and dropped mine. %616 is mine and stands. After the rebase the pin reads 36,024 bytes, headroom 4,936, rows_that_fit 2. The fleet can book a red again.
obs The reusable finding is smaller than the repair. Door B split OPEN by whether the instances stand repaired, and left unsplit the question of who a row waits on. Measured across the rows OPEN at my cold read: three carry a clause naming their holder, ten end on a bare OPEN marker. A lap reading the pin cannot tell a row awaiting Keaton's word from one awaiting a lap.
obs %616 prices that honestly rather than dramatically: it sat open twenty minutes, not days, and closed because a hand happened to look on the night the pin ran out. A fourth status word is Keaton's to seat; I name the reading and stop.
obs Against myself: I folded a peer's row the hour it closed. Lawful, and %675 is Diffuser's own account of its evening, now one directory deeper than the card citing it.
obs The scoped cold pass answered two reds. tracked_link was mine and self-inflicted: three files I had just written were untracked, so the links naming them resolved here and would break in a fresh clone. git add closed it, GREEN on re-run.
obs stash_record reds at unlanded=1 and is %636 standing, surfaced as a gate. I advanced it with a fact it lacked rather than opening it: stash@{0} is this tree's parked lap of 20260909.092442, and its three standing_equipment files applied over HEAD would remove 69, 17 and 156 lines later laps landed. The code is superseded; the record is the only part nothing carries. A test falls out -- a stash whose diff against HEAD deletes tracked lines is superseded on its code.
loom reds_pin=construction/REDS.md headroom_before=9 headroom_after=4936 rows_that_fit_before=0 rows_that_fit_after=2 open_rows=14 folded_by_me=1 folded_by_peer=1
loom ascii_document enforce_files=143 enforce_globbed=110 enforce_derived=33 enforce_chars=0 ratchet_files=303 ratchet_chars=3225 ceiling=3232
